INT. COURTHOUSE ELEVATOR - DAY

Brass walls. A cracked floor indicator. The car hums between floors.

ATTORNEY RENATA OKONKWO-VASS, 40s, sharp grey suit, briefcase at her ankle, presses her back against the rail. She stares at the floor numbers. Her jaw is tight.

The elevator jolts. Stops. The lights flicker.

She sighs. Jabs the call button.

CLERK (V.O.)
(through the speaker)
Maintenance.

RENATA
I'm in car three. Between six and seven.

CLERK (V.O.)
Yes, ma'am. We see you.

RENATA
I'm due in Courtroom 7 in four minutes.

CLERK (V.O.)
The judge has been informed.

She exhales. Sets down her briefcase. Rubs her temples.

RENATA
Is he angry?

CLERK (V.O.)
Ma'am?

RENATA
The judge.

CLERK (V.O.)
He said, and I quote, "Let her be late. She's earned it."

She blinks. Then, for the first time all day, she laughs.

RENATA
I won, didn't I?

CLERK (V.O.)
The jury came back ten minutes ago.

She leans her head against the wall. Closes her eyes.

RENATA
Then take your time.
